## Which Method Is Right for You: Silicone Stamper, Guide Stickers, or Freehand Brush?

Most tutorials skip straight to the steps without asking which method actually suits the person following them. That is the most important question in this whole post.

The three methods are not just different ways to do the same thing, they suit different hands, different nail shapes, and different levels of patience. Here is the breakdown.

**Silicone Stamper**
The stamper loads white gel onto a silicone pad shaped like a French tip, then transfers the shape to your nail in one press. It is the fastest method and the one most forgiving of an unsteady hand, because the pressure of the press does the work rather than your brushwork. The trade-off: getting consistent pressure takes a few practice runs, and the tip width is fixed to the size of your stamper head. If you want a very thin modern tip, look for a stamper marketed as "micro French," which deposits a finer line. Best for: beginners, anyone who finds freehand brushwork difficult.

**Guide Stickers (Nail Guide Stickers)**
These are curved sticker strips placed at the smile line. You cure your base colour, apply the sticker, paint white gel over the tip, cure, then peel. The sticker creates a clean edge and ensures both hands match. The method is slower (you are placing stickers on ten nails), but it produces the most consistent result for beginners and is particularly good for square or squoval shapes. Best for: beginners who want symmetry and do not mind the extra steps. If you prefer a razor-thin tip, that is the <a href='/blog/french-nails/micro-french-nails'>micro French look</a>, and the guide sticker method works beautifully for it with a light hand.

**Freehand Brush**
Drawing the smile line freehand with a fine liner or detail brush takes practice but gives you the most control over shape, thickness, and placement. If you want a thin modern line rather than a classic thick band, this is the method that lets you customise. The key is gel paint (thicker, stays put) rather than standard gel polish (thinner, runs). Best for: those who have done nails before and want precision results. Not recommended as a first attempt.

If you want to start lower-commitment while you figure out gel, <a href='/blog/french-nails/how-to-do-french-manicure-at-home'>regular polish French tips at home</a> are a good starting point before investing in a lamp and gel kit.

