## Nail Prep: Why Most DIY Gel Manicures Fail Before the First Coat

Skip prep. That is the whole explanation for why gel lifts at the edges after five days.

Gel needs a clean, dry, slightly roughened nail plate to bond to. Oil, moisture, dust, or a shiny surface all compromise that bond. The prep stage takes five minutes and determines whether your manicure lasts five days or three weeks. For ongoing nail health between gel sets, <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/topics/nail-care-guide' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>the complete nail care guide</a> covers everything worth knowing.

**Prep steps (do all of these, in this order):**

1. Remove any old polish completely with acetone
2. File nails to your chosen shape, do not file after gel is on
3. Push back cuticles gently with a cuticle pusher, do not cut
4. Lightly buff the nail plate with a 220-grit buffer until the shine is gone (10 to 15 seconds per nail is enough. you are dulling the surface, not thinning the nail)
5. Brush off all dust with a clean brush
6. Wipe each nail with a nail dehydrator or lint-free wipe saturated in alcohol, let it dry completely before touching the nail plate
7. Apply a thin layer of gel base coat, cure for 30 to 60 seconds under your LED lamp

The inhibition layer, the slightly tacky surface left after curing base coat, is intentional. That tackiness is what the next layer grips. Do not wipe it off.

