## Step-by-Step: How to Freehand a Gel French Tip (And Keep It Steady)

The "good hand and bad hand" problem is real. Every person who has attempted a freehand French tip knows exactly what that means.

The freehand method on your non-dominant hand is hard, genuinely harder than tutorials admit. The technique below is designed specifically for that problem.

**What you need:** fine liner brush or detail brush (10 mm), white gel paint or thick white gel polish, gel base coat, sheer nude gel, no-wipe top coat, LED lamp, <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/topics/at-home-hacks' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>household tip alternatives</a> like scotch tape also work as a guide

**Steps:**

1. Apply and cure gel base coat, then two thin coats of nude gel, cure between each layer
2. Decant a small amount of white gel paint onto a nail art palette or the edge of a piece of foil
3. Load the brush with a thin, even amount, wipe off excess on the palette edge
4. **Before drawing the smile line, place a tiny dot of gel at each corner of where you want the line to start.** This is the technique that fixes the asymmetry problem. Mark both corners first, then connect them.
5. Draw from the left dot inward to the centre of the nail, creating half the curve
6. Draw from the right dot inward to meet it, two strokes, not one continuous line
7. **Flash cure immediately** after each nail, this freezes the line before it can shift or smudge while you work on the next finger
8. Once all tips are placed and flash cured, go back and fill in any gaps or thin areas with a second pass
9. Full cure 60 seconds
10. Cap the free edge with no-wipe top coat, cure 60 seconds

On the non-dominant hand: rest your painting hand's elbow on the table to stabilise your wrist, and move the nail toward the brush rather than moving the brush. That small mechanical shift makes an outsized difference to line control.

