## How to Get a Perfect Smile Line and What to Do When It Goes Wrong

Two millimetres. That is the width of a modern gel French tip at its most wearable. The thick white band of the 1990s reads dated now, the current approach is narrow, precise, and sits close to the free edge.

**Getting the smile line right:**

- Mark both corners before connecting. this is the single most effective technique for symmetry
- Work on one hand at a time and compare nails within that hand before curing
- Flash cure each nail as you finish it, once cured, the line is locked and you can correct the next nail without smudging the previous one

**When the smile line goes wrong, and it will, at least once:**

*Wobbly or uneven line (not yet cured):* Wipe immediately with a lint-free wipe and start again. Gel is forgiving before curing. The whole nail does not need to come off, just wipe the white from the tip area only and re-apply.

*Tip too wide or too high on one nail:* Use a fine brush dipped in acetone and draw along the inner edge of the smile line to sharpen and reshape it before curing. Work slowly, a tiny amount of acetone on a fine brush dissolves the gel edge precisely.

*Already cured and you hate it:* You cannot dissolve a cured tip, but you can file it back. Use a fine buffer across the tip edge to reduce the width of the white band, wipe clean, and apply a corrective layer of white gel over the top, flash cure, and seal with top coat.

The smile line is the hardest part of <strong>how to do french tip gel nails</strong> consistently, even experienced nail artists use guides for both hands. There is no shame in the sticker.

