## Flash Cure, Full Cure, No-Wipe Top Coat: The Curing Steps That Actually Matter

Gel is only as good as its cure. Under-cured gel is soft, peels, and can cause skin sensitivity. Over-cured gel can yellow or become brittle. Neither extreme is what you want.

**The three curing moments that matter:**

**Flash cure (10–15 seconds):** Used after tip painting to freeze the white gel line before it moves. Not a full cure, it sets the surface without fully hardening through. Use this immediately after each nail when doing tips. The flash cure is the technique most tutorials skip entirely, and it is the reason tips smudge when moving between fingers.

**Full cure (60 seconds LED, 2 minutes UV):** Applied to every layer, base coat, colour coats, and top coat. Do not rush this. If your lamp is older or lower wattage, add 15 to 20 seconds to each cure. For reference on cure times by gel type and wattage, <a href='https://glitterbels-us.com/blogs/news/an-ultimate-guide-to-curing-gel-polish' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>this cure time guide</a> covers the variance by product type.

**Sealing the free edge:** Before curing your top coat, run the brush lightly along the very tip edge of the nail, what nail techs call "capping the free edge." This seals the gel at the point where lifting almost always begins. It takes two seconds and adds days to your wear time.

**The inhibition layer:** After curing each gel layer, the surface will feel slightly tacky. This is the inhibition layer, the uncured top surface that allows the next layer to bond. Do not wipe it between colour layers. Only wipe after a standard top coat (not no-wipe). For French tips, a no-wipe formula means you do not need to wipe at all, and the finish is glass-smooth straight out of the lamp.

