## How to Remove Gel French Tip Nails at Home Without Wrecking Your Nails

The damage from gel nails is almost never the gel itself. It is the removal, specifically, peeling.

Peeling soak-off gel removes the top layer of the nail plate with it. Do that a few times and you will have thin, bendy, fragile nails that feel almost papery. The soak method takes 15 minutes and prevents all of that.

**Soak-off removal (the correct method):**

1. Lightly buff the top coat with a coarse file (100 grit). You are breaking the seal, not filing down to the nail
2. Soak a lint-free wipe in acetone and place it flat on the nail
3. Wrap each finger in a small square of foil, the foil holds the acetone in contact with the gel
4. Wait 10 to 15 minutes, do not unwrap and check; every time you do, you let the acetone evaporate and add five minutes to the process
5. Unwrap one finger and gently push the softened gel off with a cuticle pusher, it should slide, not scrape
6. If any gel resists, rewrap for another five minutes rather than forcing it
7. Wipe clean, apply nail oil, and let your nails breathe for a day before the next set

For the dermatologist-endorsed approach to protecting your nail plate during removal, the <a href='https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/nail-care-secrets/basics/pedicures/removing-gel-polish' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>American Academy of Dermatology gel removal guidance</a> is the clearest reference available.

